\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Wilson mass and the hopping parameter}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{For the subfamily #{c_W=ra/2}, direct expansion of the [massive Wilson convention](fdip-00IN) gives
##{aK_U=(am+rd)I-\tfrac12\sum_j\bigl((r-\gamma_j)T_j^U+(r+\gamma_j)(T_j^U)^{-1}\bigr).}}

\p{For #{r=1}, division by #{am+d} gives
##{\begin{aligned}
\frac{aK_U}{am+d}&=I-\kappa\sum_j\bigl((I_S-\gamma_j)T_j^U
 +(I_S+\gamma_j)(T_j^U)^{-1}\bigr),\\
\kappa&=\frac1{2(am+d)}.
\end{aligned}} In #{d=4}, #{m>0} implies #{0<\kappa<1/8<1/6}. This checks the numerical hopping inequality used in the fermionic transfer comparison in \ref{fdip-00JB}; identifying a transfer kernel also requires its spin, gauge, boundary and observable data.}
